  "beats": [
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:01Z",
      "text": "The Day of the Dead is traditionally celebrated on November 1 and 2, though other days, such as October 31 or November 6, may be included depending on the locality.",
      "cites": [
        "wiki:rev:1380361744:0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:08Z",
      "text": "At midnight on November 2, people light candles and ride winged boats called mariposas, or butterflies, to Janitzio, an island in the middle of the lake with a cemetery, to honor the dead.",
      "cites": [
        "wiki:rev:1380361744:117"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:15Z",
      "text": "In Italy, November 2 is All Souls' Day, and it is colloquially known as Day of the Dead or Giorno dei Morti.",
      "cites": [
        "wiki:rev:1380361744:160"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:33Z",
      "text": "English Wikipedia had 63,975 readers of the article on 2 November 2026, about 13× the usual.",
      "cites": [
        "views:en:Day_of_the_Dead:2026-11-02",
        "views:en:Day_of_the_Dead:prior"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:39Z",
      "text": "The article was read in 11 languages, and after English, the most readers were in Spanish, with 19,498.",
      "cites": [
        "views:en:Day_of_the_Dead:2026-11-02",
        "views:es:Día_de_Muertos:2026-11-02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:45Z",
      "text": "On the globe, each language glows where it is mainly spoken, brighter for more readers, and a language is not a country.",
      "cites": [
        "views:es:Día_de_Muertos:2026-11-02"
      ]
    }
  ],
